\subsubsection{Scaling Experiment}
\subsubsection{Scale Experiment}

The scale experiment uses the same simulation design as the toy experiment but increases population size to test whether leadership emergence persists at larger \(n\). We use \(n=100\) here.

\textbf{Environment.}
There are two states (sampled uniformly) and two actions, with heterogeneous reward functions across agents.

\textbf{Agent estimates.}
Each agent maintains:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \(P_i[j]\): personal evaluation of agent \(j\),
  \item \(R_i[j]\): accumulated reputation estimate for agent \(j\),
  \item a policy network updated by policy gradient.
\end{itemize}
The follow condition is
\[
\gamma\,R_i[j^*] > P_i[i].
\]

\textbf{Follower dynamics.}
Influencer switching occurs every 3000 timesteps. Gossip (global averaging of reputation estimates) starts at \(t=50\). Chain-following is not allowed.

