\subsubsection{Replication Experiment}

The replication experiment runs \texttt{train.py} directly, using a more complex setup than the toy experiment.

\textbf{Setup differences from toy experiment.}
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{State space}: 512 states encoded as 9-bit binary vectors (vs.\ 2 states in the toy experiment). The policy network takes a 9-dimensional binary input.
  \item \textbf{Reward function}: Unimodal variance reward function across agents (vs.\ fixed state-optimal reward in the toy experiment). Agents have heterogeneous reward functions drawn from a distribution centered between \texttt{bottom\_reward} and \texttt{top\_reward}.
  \item \textbf{Follow rule}: \texttt{norm\_agent.py} uses \texttt{independent\_beta} as the follow threshold instead of $P_{i,i}$. Operationally, \texttt{independent\_beta} is an EMA of the agent's own received reward, but it is \emph{frozen when the agent is a follower} — it is only updated when \texttt{target\_influencer == -1}. By contrast, $P_{i,i}$ in the paper is continuously updated regardless of role. This is a known deviation from the paper formula; only \texttt{toy\_agent.py} has been corrected.
  \item \textbf{Scale}: $N=100$ agents, $\gamma=9$, 15000 timesteps.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Results.}
With $\kappa=1$ (status optimization enabled), a single opinion leader (Agent 56) with 99 followers emerges and the leadership is stable:

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{sep5_async_3000_1K_9G_90.0_b0_3.5_sep_1__roles.png}
    \caption{Roles over time: $\gamma=9$, $\kappa=1$. A single stable leader emerges.}
    \label{fig:replication-kappa1}
\end{figure}

With $\kappa=0$ (status optimization disabled), the result is not replicated — no stable single leader emerges:

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{sep5_async_3000_0K_9G_90.0_b0_3.5_sep_1__roles.png}
    \caption{Roles over time: $\gamma=9$, $\kappa=0$. No stable leader emerges.}
    \label{fig:replication-kappa0}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Discussion.}
The failure at $\kappa=0$ is notable because Alex's report sets $\kappa=0$ for reputation scaling experiments. Several factors may explain the discrepancy:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{State space complexity}: With 512 states, agents require more time to learn a reliable policy. In the toy experiment, a 2-state environment converges quickly; here, agents may not distinguish high-reputation agents reliably before leadership needs to crystallize.
  \item \textbf{Follow rule deviation}: The \texttt{independent\_beta} threshold (frozen during followership) may prevent agents from updating their baseline correctly, making the follow condition less reliable in the absence of status incentives ($\kappa=0$).
  \item \textbf{Status optimization as a stabilizer}: $\kappa=1$ provides additional gradient signal pushing influencers to maintain prosocial behavior, which may be necessary to sustain leadership in the complex environment even though the theory treats it as separable.
\end{enumerate}

The toy experiment (2 states, corrected follow rule) successfully replicates the $\gamma\ge3$ threshold with $\kappa=0$, suggesting the complexity gap — not just the $\kappa$ parameter — contributes to the replication failure.

